<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">François Mitterrand</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1996">1996</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">François Mitterrand was a French Socialist politician and president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1981 to 1995, the longest-serving president of the Fifth Republic. His election brought the left to power for the first time under that constitution; he abolished the death penalty, decentralised government and, after early nationalisations, turned to economic austerity. He twice governed in 'cohabitation' with right-wing prime ministers and championed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-integration">European integration</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francois-mitterrand.jpg" alt="François Mitterrand" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Comet Photo AG (Zürich) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Fran%C3%A7ois_Mitterrand_in_1983_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>François Mitterrand was born on 26 October 1916 in Jarnac, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charente</a>, into a devoutly Catholic and conservative family; his father was a stationmaster. He studied at the École Libre des Sciences Politiques in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Called up in 1938, he was wounded by shrapnel on 14 June 1940 and captured by the Germans on 21 June. He escaped from a prisoner-of-war camp on 28 November 1941 with forged papers, and then worked as a mid-level official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> government looking after prisoners of war. In 1943 he built up a resistance network of former prisoners. His wartime conduct caused much controversy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the 1980s and 1990s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under the Fourth Republic Mitterrand held ministerial office several times, among them Minister of the Interior in the cabinet of Pierre Mendès-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1954–1955, when he had to direct the response to the Algerian War of Independence. In 1958 he was one of the few who opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>'s plan for a Fifth Republic, and his book Le Coup d'État permanent (1964) criticised de Gaulle's personal power. In 1971 he became first secretary of the Socialist Party, arguing that an alliance with the Communists was necessary to win power. He was the left's standard bearer in 1965 and 1974.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 10 May 1981 Mitterrand became the first socialist President of the Fifth Republic. His first term began with nationalisation of key firms and a 39-hour work week, alongside the abolition of the death penalty, but facing economic tensions he soon turned to austerity and market liberalisation. In July 1985 French intelligence agents sank the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenpeace">Greenpeace</a> ship Rainbow Warrior in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a>, killing a photographer.</p><p>He was re-elected in 1988, and twice governed in cohabitation with conservative cabinets led by Jacques Chirac and Édouard Balladur. With the German chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmut-kohl">Helmut Kohl</a> he advanced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-integration">European integration</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht-treaty">Maastricht Treaty</a>. He left office in 1995 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 8 January 1996 of prostate cancer, which had been concealed for most of his presidency.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “François Mitterrand” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1980s <b>François Mitterrand</b>’s government tried to nationalise all French banks.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-democracy">Social democracy · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Queen Elizabeth II and President <b>François Mitterrand</b> opened the 50 km rail tunnel under the Channel.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-6">6 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>François Mitterrand's</b> election in 1981 brought the left to power, and periods of cohabitation followed.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-fifth-republic">French Fifth Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Maison natale de François Mitterrand, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in 7th arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.68,-0.18&amp;year=1968" aria-label="Maison natale de François Mitterrand on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of François Mitterrand"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4989 6212h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4989 6212h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.68,-0.18&amp;year=1968">What was here in 1968? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
